{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with only one depth stratum support lower species richness per transect than reefs with two depth strata.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the number of depth strata at a reef (one vs. two) influences local species richness, controlling for reef identity.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, count unique depth values to classify reefs as “One-Stratum” or “Two-Strata.”\n2. For each transect, compute species richness = count of unique species observed.\n3. Merge richness with the reef stratum category.\n4. Fit a mixed-effects model: richness ~ stratum_category + (1 | id_reef).\n5. Extract and test the fixed-effect coefficient for stratum_category.\n6. Plot boxplots of species richness by stratum category with predicted means.\n7. Check model residuals for homoscedasticity and normality.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-effects model summary showing effect of stratum category on richness. 2. Boxplot of richness by stratum category with model-predicted means. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Transect-level species richness decreases as mean sea-surface temperature (SST) increases, independent of reef identity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between local SST and species richness, controlling for reef-level variation.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute species richness per transect.\n2. Retrieve mean_sst for each transect.\n3. Fit a mixed-effects model: richness ~ mean_sst + (1 | id_reef).\n4. Test the significance of the mean_sst coefficient.\n5. Visualize richness vs. mean_sst scatter with the fitted regression line (conditional on average random effect).\n6. Perform residual diagnostics for normality and heteroscedasticity.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary for richness ~ mean_sst. 2. Scatterplot of richness vs. mean_sst with mixed-model fit. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shannon diversity index is significantly lower in deep (20 m) transects than shallow (5 m) transects, after accounting for reef identity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare community evenness (Shannon index) between depth strata while controlling for reef-level differences.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect, compute species proportions p_i = quantity_i / total_quantity.\n2. Calculate Shannon index H = -∑ p_i ln(p_i).\n3. Label each transect by depth stratum (Shallow vs Deep).\n4. Fit a mixed-effects model: H ~ C(depth) + (1 | id_reef).\n5. Test if the depth effect (Deep vs Shallow) is significant.\n6. Plot boxplots of H by depth with predicted means overlaid.\n7. Diagnose model residuals for normality and homogeneity.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-model summary for H ~ depth. 2. Boxplot of Shannon index by depth with model predictions. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The coefficient of variation (CV) of fish individual size per transect increases with higher mean SST, controlling for reef identity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether warmer waters are associated with greater size heterogeneity in reef fish communities.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect, compute weighted mean size and weighted standard deviation of size (weights = quantity).\n2. Calculate CV_size = sd_size / mean_size.\n3. Extract mean_sst for each transect.\n4. Fit a mixed-effects model: CV_size ~ mean_sst + (1 | id_reef).\n5. Test the mean_sst coefficient for significance.\n6. Plot CV_size vs. mean_sst with the fitted line and random-intercept adjustment.\n7. Check residuals for normality and variance patterns.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary for CV_size ~ mean_sst. 2. Scatterplot of CV_size against mean_sst with fitted relationship. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Biomass density per transect exhibits a temporal trend over survey years, after controlling for reef identity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Detect whether there is a significant increase or decrease in fish biomass density over time across all reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect, compute biomass_density = biomass / (area / 10000) to get t/ha.\n2. Extract survey year for each transect.\n3. Fit a mixed-effects model: biomass_density ~ year + (1 | id_reef).\n4. Test whether the year coefficient differs from zero.\n5. Plot biomass_density vs. year with fitted mixed-model line.\n6. Perform residual diagnostics.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-model summary showing year effect on biomass density. 2. Time-series plot of biomass density with model fit. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within-reef species composition turnover (Jaccard dissimilarity) between consecutive years increases as SST anomaly between those years increases.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Link interannual community turnover to anomalous SST changes within reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Aggregate presence/absence of species per reef-year across all transects.\n2. For each reef, pair consecutive years (year t and t+1).\n3. Compute Jaccard dissimilarity for each pair: 1 – (|A∩B|/|A∪B|).\n4. Compute SST anomaly = mean_sst_{t+1} – mean_sst_t for that reef.\n5. Fit a linear mixed-effects model: jaccard ~ sst_anomaly + (1 | id_reef).\n6. Test significance of sst_anomaly coefficient.\n7. Plot turnover vs. SST anomaly with fitted line and random effects.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary of turnover ~ SST anomaly. 2. Scatterplot of Jaccard dissimilarity vs SST anomaly with fitted mixed-model line. 3. Table of reef-level turnover statistics."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The bias of biomass density from transect area deviation is magnified in warmer waters: the interaction of area deviation and SST is significant.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the linear bias correction for transect area deviations varies with mean SST.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute biomass_density and area_deviation = area – 250 for each transect.\n2. Retrieve mean_sst for each transect.\n3. Fit a mixed-effects model: biomass_density ~ area_deviation * mean_sst + (1 | id_reef).\n4. Test the interaction term for significance.\n5. Generate contour or interaction plots showing biomass_density bias across combinations of area_deviation and SST.\n6. Diagnose residuals.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-model summary including interaction term. 2. Interaction plot of predicted bias vs. area deviation at low, medium, and high SST. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The skewness of the fish size distribution per transect differs between shallow and deep strata, controlling for reef identity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether size distributions are more right- or left-skewed in shallow vs deep transects.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect, expand entries by quantity and compute skewness of size (e.g., using scipy.stats.skew).\n2. Label each transect by depth stratum.\n3. Fit a mixed-effects model: skewness_size ~ C(depth) + (1 | id_reef).\n4. Test the fixed effect of depth on skewness.\n5. Plot violin or boxplots of skewness by depth with predicted means.\n6. Perform residual diagnostics.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary for skewness_size ~ depth. 2. Plot of skewness by depth with model predictions. 3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  }  
]}
